63
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.460
And Angela makes it clear to Dwight that they are done.

64
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.880
Yeah, she is not forgiving him.

65
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.640
Well, gang, since we did fast facts on the first half of this episode, and since it is

66
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.980
a new year, we thought we should pay a visit to our mailbag and answer some questions.

67
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.740
Yes, it's time to talk to you guys.

68
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.640
Well, our first letter came from Jessie S, and she said,

69
00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:01.940
Hi, I am a hard of hearing person who loves to listen to the podcast, but I do miss some

70
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.320
things as I rely on lip reading a lot.

71
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.680
I am wondering if transcribing the podcast is an option for other deaf and hard of hearing

72
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.720
who are fans of the show and would love to be able to interact with the podcaSt.

73
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:19.940
Well, guys, we do have a transcription for every single episode going back to the pilot,

74
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.800
and you can find it on officeladies.com.

75
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.260
If you go to the episodes and scroll down, there's a little button for transcript.

76
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:33.520
Yes, you go past the sort of summary of the episode, and it's right there, and you can

77
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.900
click on it and read the whole thing.

78
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.680
This is kind of personal to me, Jessie, because my sister Emily wears hearing aids, and she

79
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:47.960
also relies on lip reading, and so I know that sometimes being able to both listen and read

80
00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.300
along is really helpful for her.

81
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.240
And also, like when I go to her house, she always has the closed caption on her TV.

82
00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.640
So we definitely, definitely want the podcast to be accessible to everyone.

83
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.260
So guys, if there's any other ways we can make it more accessible, let us know.

84
00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:10.120
Yes, and in the meantime, go to officeladies.com and go to each episode and scroll down, and

85
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.380
the transcript is right there, and we have a team here at Earwolf that helps us with that,

86
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.520
and we so appreciate it.

87
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.540
All right, well, our next question, Jenna, comes from Julie Weiss, and she says,

88
00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.200
I have a question about kissing the other actors.

89
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:30.060
How do you prep for kissing someone that you are friends with, but not romantically involved

90
00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:30.360
with?

91
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.020
Was it ever awkward?

92
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.240
Oh, Julie.

93
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.700
Julie, it's always awkward.

94
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.020
It's always awkward.

95
00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.640
And thank goodness over the years, though, you do become such good friends that it's just

96
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.340
like, okay, well, we'll just do this really quickly.

97
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.940
But something that made it difficult for me, which maybe you wouldn't think about, is that

98
00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.660
I often had to kiss Rainn Wilson, and for whatever reason, he was usually eating something

99
00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.440
disgusting right beforehand.

100
00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.680
And I'd be like, Rainn, dang it.

101
00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.120
Do you have to eat a tuna fish sandwich right before we're supposed to kiss?

102
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:07.880
Come on.

103
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.040
Yeah, I remember.

104
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.340
He would always be like downing a cup of coffee and an everything bagel.

105
00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:14.660
Yes.

106
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.380
And he'd be like, I'm ready for my kissing scene.

107
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.180
And you would be like, Rainn, a mint.

108
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.800
Can you do something?

109
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.420
Anything at this point.

110
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.140
Just not the everything bagel, you know?

111
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:27.480
My God.

112
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.280
Well, at least for me, John was always very conscientious.

113
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:32.460
He would have a mint.

114
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:33.220
Thank you.

115
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.580
Or some gum.

116
00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:35.560
A mint.

117
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.340
Rainn Wilson, a mint.

118
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.680
We were very breath aware.

119
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.560
Rainn, not so much.

120
00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:48.820
Also, Julie, we didn't do the big open mouth stuff that maybe you see in movies and whatnot.

121
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.440
This was like television comedy.

122
00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:55.720
Kissing is very different than like a dramatic movie.

123
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.960
Make out, you know, it's your lips are kind of just together.

124
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.320
You know, you kind of fake it.

125
00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:03.100
Fakey kiss, fakey bag.

126
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.720
Well, our next question comes from Jessica Willis.

127
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.680
I've always been curious, how much ADR did you have to typically do for your episodes?

128
00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:20.120
I've noticed that in some episodes, you see characters remove a mic pack and the audio goes black.

129
00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:24.020
It's such a real cool detail and a reference to the documentary.

130
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.040
So how much original audio did you use and how much was re-recorded after filming?

131
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.480
Well, lady, my memory is we did not do a lot of ADr.

132
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.880
I mean, I have one memory of the whole nine years of going in to do it.

133
00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:40.480
Yeah.

134
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:45.220
In the entire nine years, I only recorded one line that they had to dub in later.

135
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.500
One in nine years.

136
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.400
And I thought that's how it worked.

137
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:49.180
Yeah.

138
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.800
And when I went on other jobs, part of your job is you go in a studio every week and you

139
00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:03.120
dub lines that got missed or that maybe a plane flew over your head or there was a sound problem

140
00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.060
and they dump it in.

141
00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:09.880
And I'd spend maybe 30 minutes, an hour just doing that on other shows.

142
00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.340
But on our show, we did not.

143
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:12.420
And why is that, Jenna?

144
00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.260
Because same, all the other projects I've gone on, you have like an ADR day where you go in.

145
00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:22.040
But on the office, I only did ADR once and it was for the finale.

146
00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.740
And I got to see footage of the finale before it aired, before anyone saw it, because I

147
00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.620
went in and had to loop one line.

148
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:34.400
And it was because we were outside and, you know, we had that storm coming in and they

149
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.960
just needed me to loop a line.

150
00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.160
And I even took a picture in the ADR booth because I'd never been in there.

151
00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:46.940
You know, first of all, our sound engineers were Emmy award winning.

152
00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.580
They won Emmys for their sound design on our show.

153
00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:50.680
They're just amazing.

154
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.700
But they also had a mic pack on every single actor.

155
00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.800
We had two boom operators in the room and they hid microphones throughout the office.

156
00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:00.940
I had one at reception.

157
00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:01.640
Oh, yeah.

158
00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:07.480
We had a microphone in the accounting pod and they put it next to Oscar's phone.

159
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:14.420
So it would sort of pick up the three of us, you know, but I also remember I would often

160
00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:20.820
see our boom operators and camera operators having little powwows because they would have

161
00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:26.800
to choreograph the boom swing with the camera swing so that they could pick up the sound.

162
00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:31.460
And it was this crafted dance that they did.

163
00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.260
And it's really amazing.

164
00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:37.520
And I'm actually glad that Jessica wrote in because it gives us a chance to really shout

165
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.500
out our sound department, which was incredible.

166
00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:44.120
Our main guys that were with us on the stage were Ben Patrick.

167
00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:45.720
Yep.

168
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.360
He was our main like production sound mixer, sound editor.

169
00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.540
And we had our two boom operators, Nick Carbone.

170
00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:54.700
Don't get Carbone.

171
00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.540
Don't get Carbone.

172
00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:03.480
Sound and Brian Whittle, Brian Whittle would be the inspiration for the boom operator that

173
00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.400
we see later in the story.

174
00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:06.840
Yeah.

175
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:12.080
But then we also had like a mixing department and we had other boom operators and people

176
00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:12.980
that would rotate in.

177
00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.940
We just had a really amazing sound team.

178
00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:16.120
Yeah, they were great.

179
00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:17.980
Well, we have one more question.

180
00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.920
This one is from Alicia W.

181
00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.440
She asked, in the talking heads where the camera is facing into the office, Stanley is

182
00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.360
almost always in the background.

183
00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.120
Would Leslie David Baker have to stay in the background the whole time they filmed talking

184
00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.900
heads or was that background edited in?

185
00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.380
Oh, Alicia, he had to sit there.

186
00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:39.000
Yes.

187
00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:40.440
For nine years.

188
00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.780
Poor Leslie David Baker.

189
00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.980
He was always in the background.

190
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.540
He was always there.

191
00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:51.680
You could see him at his desk and he was such a trooper.

192
00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.020
When people had talking heads, when we had a long day of them, he'd sit there.

193
00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:56.420
Yep.

194
00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:00.640
And sometimes Phyllis would have to sit there and sometimes Creed as well.

195
00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:01.940
Yes.

196
00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:08.060
And early on, they would have people there designated to cross.

197
00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.080
Like you might see someone like cross to the kitchen or cross back.

198
00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.740
We abandoned that after a couple of seasons.

199
00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:20.300
I think if you look at early season talking heads, you see crosses in the background.

200
00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:25.020
And then at a certain point, people were like, I think I'm thinking, no, I think I'm not

201
00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:28.560
going to go to the kitchen and back to get a cup of coffee for two hours.

202
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:29.260
Yeah.

203
00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:29.800
I don't.

204
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:34.280
Also, I think they're a little distracting because I know I did a cross one time and they

205
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.820
had me cross really close to the conference room, that cabinet that was there.

206
00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:38.800
And then they're like, no.

207
00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.880
And then they had me cross way in the back.

208
00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.000
But I think ultimately they're just like, OK, no, no crossing.

209
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:48.280
Well, also, if you wanted to improv, it would throw off the timing of the cross.

210
00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:48.600
Yeah.

211
00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.180
And so you would when you went into editing, it did not work for crosses.

212
00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.440
It just worked to have poor Leslie at his desk.

213
00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:55.700
Yes.

214
00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:56.500
As a constant.

215
00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:58.520
Leslie and then Creed.

216
00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.920
I think Creed would be the number two runner up for that.

217
00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:02.440
I think so, too.

218
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:07.760
And then before we go to break, we just got a really nice comment and it made us happy.

219
00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:08.800
So we thought we'd share it.

220
00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:10.380
It is from Cecilia B.

221
00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:15.160
And she says, ladies, I have to tell you, you two are amazing.

222
00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.720
Thank you, Cecilia.

223
00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.100
I finally got the courage to ask for our changes at work.

