Well.

1018
00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:28.560
Which Randall knew about from Survivor.

1019
00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:31.200
Because remember, he spent all that time on Survivor.

1020
00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:31.920
Yes.

1021
00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:35.020
He said they also put mics in the visors.

1022
00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:39.440
And they had boom operators standing outside of the car in water.

1023
00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:44.560
So, our boom guys were, like, in, like, those, like, rubber pants in the water.

1024
00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:52.220
Randall confirmed that they did three takes, but they were losing daylight on the last one.

1025
00:47:52.360 --> 00:48:00.320
And he's pretty certain that we used the first take because everybody's reactions to hitting the water were just so pure.

1026
00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:00.740
Yeah.

1027
00:48:02.100 --> 00:48:05.280
And he said that there are only two cut points in the scene.

1028
00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:09.940
So, the first cut point is when the camera pans down to the GPS.

1029
00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:14.560
That's when they cut into the car being on the rig.

1030
00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:15.980
I mean, you can't even tell.

1031
00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:16.240
No.

1032
00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:16.600
It's seamless.

1033
00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:18.220
I watched it, like, five times.

1034
00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:19.720
And I couldn't figure out.

1035
00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:20.660
They did a great job.

1036
00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:21.200
How they did it.

1037
00:48:22.100 --> 00:48:30.080
And he said, and then after that point, it is all one shot until you see the shot of the basket floating in the water.

1038
00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:36.500
And that's when they cut away and they reset to get all the stuff of Michael and Dwight crawling out of the lake.

1039
00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:45.540
But all of that commotion of getting out of the car and, like, Steve hanging on to the passenger side window and opening the door, that was all one take.

1040
00:48:45.780 --> 00:48:48.440
And that was what Randall was fighting for.

1041
00:48:48.660 --> 00:48:52.660
He was saying lipstick cams could have never, ever picked up.

1042
00:48:52.820 --> 00:48:53.760
Captured that.

1043
00:48:54.260 --> 00:48:54.740
Yeah.

1044
00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:55.760
I have a question.

1045
00:48:56.140 --> 00:49:03.100
As Steve is making his way out and he's clinging on to the side of the car, he opens the back passenger door.

1046
00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:05.400
And was that to let Randall out?

1047
00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:08.480
Well, we had a lot of people write in about that.

1048
00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:13.060
They said, is Michael trying to save the camera operator or the gift basket?

1049
00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:13.380
Yeah.

1050
00:49:13.980 --> 00:49:18.580
And I asked Randall and Randall said, my instinct would be the gift basket.

1051
00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:23.420
He's like, I never thought that Michael was opening the door for me.

1052
00:49:23.580 --> 00:49:27.860
But I thought, it's funny, I gave credit to Michael.

1053
00:49:28.040 --> 00:49:30.700
I thought Michael was trying to free the camera operator.

1054
00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:31.200
Yeah.

1055
00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:43.840
Well, at 35 minutes, 17 seconds, there is a absolutely perfect shot of our camera operator's shadow across the lake.

1056
00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:45.480
I saw that too.

1057
00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:49.700
I was surprised I didn't see boom mic shadows.

1058
00:49:49.700 --> 00:49:52.440
I was looking for boom shadows and I didn't see them.

1059
00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:53.140
I was impressed.

1060
00:49:53.980 --> 00:49:56.860
But yes, the camera operator's shadow is there.

1061
00:49:57.700 --> 00:50:03.820
Well, Jenna, I reached out to Rain because I was like, Rain, what was it like driving into the lake?

1062
00:50:03.820 --> 00:50:06.380
I texted him and he sent us an audio clip.

1063
00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:07.400
Sam, can you play it?

1064
00:50:07.660 --> 00:50:09.020
Hi, office ladies.

1065
00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:10.780
It's me, Rain.

1066
00:50:11.200 --> 00:50:15.060
Angela called and asked me if I had any memories about driving into the lake.

1067
00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:21.400
And my main fear wasn't like drowning or the car or anything like that.

1068
00:50:21.540 --> 00:50:27.400
It was kind of like, this water is not the cleanest and there's all these houses all over the place.

1069
00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:30.280
And I just knew it was pretty sewagey.

1070
00:50:30.400 --> 00:50:34.640
And I was just really afraid of like an eye infection or something like that.

1071
00:50:34.860 --> 00:50:38.860
It was like kind of eerily warm.

1072
00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:40.540
The water was not cold at all.

1073
00:50:41.300 --> 00:50:42.740
But it was super fun.

1074
00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:45.020
I mean, I love a chance to do a stunt.

1075
00:50:45.340 --> 00:50:50.160
I was really excited to be a part of it.

1076
00:50:50.360 --> 00:50:55.020
And I mean, how often do you get to kind of drive your car into a lake?

1077
00:50:55.120 --> 00:50:56.860
It was fantastic.

1078
00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:59.020
So much fun to shoot.

1079
00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:02.860
And I wish I had more specifics.

1080
00:51:04.180 --> 00:51:11.340
But it was just one of those kind of days where you're kind of like, wow, this is what I do for a living today.

1081
00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:16.780
I'm playing a fictional weirdo and driving my car into a lake.

1082
00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:20.040
And with Steve Carell, I mean, it was pretty great.

1083
00:51:20.420 --> 00:51:20.660
Okay.

1084
00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:21.460
I love you.

1085
00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:21.720
Goodbye.

1086
00:51:22.200 --> 00:51:25.340
I love that Rain's biggest concern was getting pink eye.

1087
00:51:26.860 --> 00:51:29.440
I don't want to get a stye today, guys.

1088
00:51:29.600 --> 00:51:30.460
Not today.

1089
00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:34.040
I would have had so many concerns doing this stunt.

1090
00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:37.120
It did kind of gross me out that he said the water was oddly warm.

1091
00:51:37.300 --> 00:51:39.760
And you have to remember, we filmed this at Malibu Lake.

1092
00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:41.080
And that is a man-made lake.

1093
00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:44.700
So there isn't like anything flowing into it.

1094
00:51:44.780 --> 00:51:46.980
You know, it is probably a little stagnant.

1095
00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:49.740
Well, I have to point something out.

1096
00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:54.960
When the tow truck pulls the car out, Jenna, the passenger door is shut.

1097
00:51:55.120 --> 00:51:56.340
But Michael opened it.

1098
00:51:56.920 --> 00:51:57.780
Oh, yeah.

1099
00:51:58.200 --> 00:52:04.360
And then the other thing I was thinking about was, did Michael and Dwight just happen to have their windows down?

1100
00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:06.320
Because they climb out through their windows.

1101
00:52:06.520 --> 00:52:08.520
I'm like, were they driving around with their windows down?

1102
00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:13.080
So there are little moments like that that were just sort of like, I was like, huh, okay, guys.

1103
00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:22.820
Well, when I was emailing with Steve about this scene, Angela, he reminded me that we got a lot of pushback after this episode aired because of this moment.

1104
00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:31.520
He said people kind of thought it was a little over the top, that it was unrealistic that someone would follow their GPS into a lake.

1105
00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:34.260
Yeah, people were like, no way, that would never happen.

1106
00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:45.500
But he told me that the writers based this on a series of real-life incidents of people following their GPS into perilous situations.

1107
00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:51.000
So I reached out to Greg, and he said this was a pitch by Jen Salata.

1108
00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:54.780
So, of course, I had to follow this trail.

1109
00:52:55.120 --> 00:52:56.720
I reached out to Jen.

1110
00:52:56.720 --> 00:52:57.800
What did Jen say?

1111
00:52:58.760 --> 00:53:04.200
She said during the hiatus before season four, she went home to visit her parents in Maryland.

1112
00:53:04.460 --> 00:53:08.320
And she was sitting in the back seat of their brand new minivan.

1113
00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:11.420
She specified it was a Honda Odyssey minivan.

1114
00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:16.640
She picked up the owner's manual and was reading about the minivan's GPS system.

1115
00:53:17.320 --> 00:53:27.260
She said that on the very first page of the manual, there was a warning that said, do not drive your car into any bodies of water, even if the GPS tells you to.

1116
00:53:28.400 --> 00:53:30.440
And Jen was like, what?

1117
00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:32.120
Who would do that?

1118
00:53:32.240 --> 00:53:36.020
You know, Honda had to put that in there because someone had done it.

1119
00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:37.140
Yes.

1120
00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:40.600
And she was like, wait a second.

1121
00:53:40.660 --> 00:53:42.260
You know who would do that?

1122
00:53:42.920 --> 00:53:44.520
Michael Scott would do that.

1123
00:53:44.940 --> 00:53:45.860
Yes, he would.

1124
00:53:45.860 --> 00:53:55.300
So, she said before she pitched it, because she was a little worried it was too crazy of an idea, she researched this phenomenon.

1125
00:53:55.540 --> 00:54:05.900
And she said there were so many cases of people doing things like driving their cars off of a cliff or into water because their GPS told them to.

1126
00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:12.120
She said usually it was a case where, like, maybe there was a bridge somewhere and it had been taken down.

1127
00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:16.440
And the GPS didn't know and the GPS didn't know and it told you to drive across a bridge that didn't exiSt.

1128
00:54:17.120 --> 00:54:24.360
But anyway, she brought it into the room and she pitched it and she showed all of her evidence for why this could really happen.

1129
00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:26.660
And it made it into the episode.

1130
00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:28.440
Well, it's fantastic.

1131
00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:32.280
And you guys, she is right because I Googled it as well.

1132
00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:40.360
There are so many stories online of people driving their cars into things because the GPS told them to.

1133
00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:45.380
Here, of all of them that I read, and there were so many, I couldn't read them all.

1134
00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:47.760
Here are two examples just to show you.

1135
00:54:47.760 --> 00:54:48.160
Okay?

1136
00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:49.200
Okay.

1137
00:54:49.540 --> 00:54:49.920
Okay.

1138
00:54:50.140 --> 00:54:59.220
In 2009, Robert Jones was using his navigation in West Yorkshire, England, and the road began to steepen and narrow.

1139
00:54:59.320 --> 00:55:01.600
But it kept telling him to keep going.

1140
00:55:01.900 --> 00:55:06.820
It kept insisting the path was the road, he later explained, so he trusted it.

1141
00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:16.100
Jones only realized how wrong he was when his car bumped up against a thin wire fence just inches from a 100-foot drop.

1142
00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:19.700
The car was taking him on the edge.

1143
00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.460
Like, the edge, there was no road.

1144
00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:27.080
He managed to get out safely, but the car remained balanced on the edge.

1145
00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:31.040
It took a recovery team nine hours to haul the car away.

1146
00:55:31.280 --> 00:55:31.900
Oh, my God.

1147
00:55:31.900 --> 00:55:32.980
So this wasn't a road.

1148
00:55:33.160 --> 00:55:38.540
It just was slowly, like, it was like a road that, like, narrowed into this, you know, hill.

1149
00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:40.340
It was like a cliffside.

1150
00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:40.820
Yes.

1151
00:55:41.140 --> 00:55:42.520
And then here's this.

1152
00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:46.520
In 2011, three women were visiting Bellevue, Washington.

1153
00:55:47.240 --> 00:55:53.840
And they were out after midnight, and they were unable to find their hotel, so they asked GPS, right?

1154
00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:56.480
That seems like a normal thing to do.

1155
00:55:57.020 --> 00:56:03.580
GPS rerouted them and took them on what they thought was a road that would lead them to the highway.

1156
00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:07.880
Instead, their SUV ended up sinking into deep water.

1157
00:56:08.100 --> 00:56:10.880
The road turned out to be a boat launch.

1158
00:56:11.560 --> 00:56:12.600
Oh, my God.

1159
00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:13.100
Yes.

1160
00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:14.660
And the water was a lake.

1161
00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:20.880
All three managed to get out safely, but by the time the tow truck arrived, the SUV was completely submerged.

1162
00:56:21.760 --> 00:56:27.140
Quote, from the—and this is the local fireman in Bellevue, Washington, was quoted as saying this.

1163
00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:32.600
Yes, we've seen sitcom parodies of something like this, but to actually see it is surprising.

1164
00:56:33.660 --> 00:56:36.200
Oh, I'm sure they were referring to our show.

1165
00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:37.180
I think so.

1166
00:56:37.740 --> 00:56:43.360
You know, when Lee and I were first dating, we went to France.

1167
00:56:43.760 --> 00:56:46.580
This was our first big trip together as a couple.

1168
00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:47.900
Oh, that sounds so fun.

1169
00:56:47.900 --> 00:56:54.620
Yes, and we drove through the French countryside, and we rented a car, but the GPS was in French.

1170
00:56:54.620 --> 00:56:55.820
We weren't thinking.

1171
00:56:56.540 --> 00:57:00.400
Well, we've discussed, Angela, that I know a little bit of French.

1172
00:57:00.520 --> 00:57:01.720
You know a handful of French.

1173
00:57:02.260 --> 00:57:05.180
So I say to Lee, I'll translate it.

1174
00:57:05.420 --> 00:57:08.480
This sounds like your first fight as a couple about to happen.

1175
00:57:09.340 --> 00:57:15.640
Honey, I'll translate the navigation in a foreign country where you're driving and you've never driven here before.

1176
00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:23.320
Well, the problem was that it would take me a second to realize what the directions were.

1177
00:57:24.580 --> 00:57:33.200
And so there was this one turn where it would be like, turn right, turn right.

1178
00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:36.500
But I was behind and we missed the turn.

1179
00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:41.980
Yeah, so Lee did this huge circle around to get us back to this spot.

1180
00:57:42.140 --> 00:57:44.960
And the person would be like, turn, eh?

1181
00:57:45.300 --> 00:57:45.880
I do what?

1182
00:57:46.040 --> 00:57:47.300
And I'd be like, right, right, right.

1183
00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:48.160
It's turn right.

1184
00:57:48.380 --> 00:57:55.440
We missed that turn like four times because of my lag in translating properly.

1185
00:57:55.560 --> 00:57:58.020
But it was more than she wasn't just saying that.

1186
00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:04.060
She was like, lots of French and names of streets and somewhere embedded in there would be like, turn, eh?

1187
00:58:04.320 --> 00:58:04.640
Adwoat.

1188
00:58:04.920 --> 00:58:07.640
And I'd be like, right, I think, right.

1189
00:58:07.900 --> 00:58:17.140
I feel like if it was you and I in the car and I'm driving and you're doing that, I'd be like, the real southerner in me would come out like, did she say Adwoat or not?

1190
00:58:21.020 --> 00:58:25.780
Well, you know Lee, Angela, and he is the most level person on the planet.

1191
00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:28.940
Oh, this man, guys, he is so well-tempered.

1192
00:58:28.940 --> 00:58:30.900
He's so good-natured.

1193
00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:31.700
Like.

1194
00:58:31.940 --> 00:58:32.500
Unshakeable.

1195
00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:33.280
Unshakeable.

1196
00:58:34.540 --> 00:58:42.500
Well, after the fourth time of me missing turn a Adwoat, he just goes, well, what the f**k?

1197
00:58:44.840 --> 00:58:46.820
I was like, oh, my God, I broke him.

1198
00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:48.300
I broke Lee.

1199
00:58:48.300 --> 00:58:50.500
Was it like the first time you ever heard him curse?

1200
00:58:50.500 --> 00:58:56.830
Yes, he was just, I was like, does he still love me after France?

1201
00:58:57.530 --> 00:59:01.750
But actually, we laughed so hard about it for that very reason.

1202
00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:06.170
Yeah, I was like, oh, my God, I broke you in France.

1203
00:59:07.050 --> 00:59:07.730
We laughed.

1204
00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:08.810
Well, you didn't break him.

1205
00:59:08.890 --> 00:59:10.310
The navigation lady broke him.

1206
00:59:10.690 --> 00:59:11.870
Oh, my gosh.

1207
00:59:12.030 --> 00:59:20.310
Guys, if you ever traveled to a foreign country and you're thinking you'll just rely on your GPS, you have to request the language.

1208
00:59:20.310 --> 00:59:28.590
Listen, listen, I won't take us down that road, but Josh and I had a fight in Scotland where he had to be driving on the other side of the road trying to do a,

1209
00:59:28.690 --> 00:59:30.230
and now for a roundabout.

1210
00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:33.430
It was like, I can't f**k roundabout.

1211
00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:34.570
Anyway.

1212
00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:36.590
Roundabouts are so great, though.

1213
00:59:36.750 --> 00:59:38.030
They really are so smart.

1214
00:59:38.250 --> 00:59:39.950
We should have roundabouts in this country.

1215
00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:42.250
They are wonderful inventions.

1216
00:59:43.370 --> 00:59:54.190
They're overwhelming at first because they are very foreign to us as American drivers, but truly they are a wonderful way to control the flow of traffic.

1217
00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:58.950
I feel myself needing to deep dive on the history of roundabouts.

1218
00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:05.970
Deep dive, it probably goes back to the Romans or something, but doing a roundabout, driving on the opposite side of the road,

1219
01:00:06.130 --> 01:00:13.110
and exiting the opposite way that you've never exited is a recipe for maybe one of your first big fights in a car.

1220
01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:13.710
Just saying.

1221
01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:20.530
Well, my pet peeve in America is that when we do have roundabouts, we put stop signs in them, which is not the point of a roundabout.

1222
01:00:20.770 --> 01:00:22.170
Well, that's not how you do it.

1223
01:00:22.170 --> 01:00:23.150
We're doing it wrong.

1224
01:00:23.750 --> 01:00:24.010
Okay.

1225
01:00:24.270 --> 01:00:27.610
Well, guys, they do get out of the lake.

1226
01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:29.570
They're soaking wet.

1227
01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:44.570
And their big takeaway from this moment, the thing that they feel determined to do is to walk back to the previous client's office and reclaim their gift basket.

1228
01:00:44.790 --> 01:00:46.410
Michael says they're going to take a stand.

1229
01:00:47.050 --> 01:00:48.950
Also, Dwight's phone works.

1230
01:00:49.510 --> 01:00:50.790
His flip phone.

1231
01:00:51.050 --> 01:00:51.270
Yeah.

1232
01:00:51.270 --> 01:00:52.110
Old tech alert.

1233
01:00:52.310 --> 01:00:55.450
What I didn't understand about this was why they couldn't just take a cab.

1234
01:00:56.530 --> 01:00:57.550
They have to walk.

1235
01:00:57.710 --> 01:00:57.890
Yeah.

1236
01:00:58.270 --> 01:00:59.270
I don't know.

1237
01:00:59.570 --> 01:01:02.130
I don't know because Dwight's phone does work.

1238
01:01:02.390 --> 01:01:04.610
But Michael's like, no, we're walking.

1239
01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:14.170
Well, back at the office, Ryan is reviewing some of Pam's sketches for the new Dunder Mifflin Infinity logo, which, by the way, I thought were great.

1240
01:01:14.850 --> 01:01:15.170
Yeah.

1241
01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:16.650
She did a great job.

1242
01:01:16.650 --> 01:01:19.950
But that's not what really Ryan was there to talk about.

1243
01:01:20.770 --> 01:01:21.290
No.

1244
01:01:21.470 --> 01:01:23.190
He wasn't really getting her sketches.

1245
01:01:23.190 --> 01:01:26.790
He just wants to take her out to dinner, and he was trying to be sneaky.

